description:
The `truncate_text` function uses `&text[..max_chars]` to slice bytes directly. If max_chars lands mid-character in a multi-byte UTF-8 sequence (emoji, CJK, etc.), this panics at runtime.

**Locations affected:**
- `letta-server/src/tools/agent_advanced/mod.rs` (lines 59-70)
- `letta-server/src/tools/agent_advanced/messaging.rs` (lines 49-57) - message text truncation
- `letta-server/src/tools/mcp_ops.rs` (lines 405-419) - func_return truncation

**Proposed fix:**
Use `is_char_boundary()` to find a valid boundary:
```rust
fn truncate_text(text: &str, max_chars: usize) -> String {
    if text.len() <= max_chars {
        return text.to_string();
    }
    let mut end = max_chars;
    while end > 0 && !text.is_char_boundary(end) {
        end -= 1;
    }
    format!("{}...[truncated]", &text[..end])
}
```

In `letta-server/src/tools/memory_unified/search.rs` (lines 186-191), byte-level string slicing is used:

```rust
let truncated_content = if content.len() > 500 {
    format!("{}...", &content[..500])
} else {
    content
};
```

**Problem:**
- `&content[..500]` will panic if position 500 falls within a multi-byte UTF-8 character
- Similar issue to the critical UTF-8 truncation bug elsewhere

**Proposed fix:**
Use a char-aware truncation approach with `is_char_boundary()`.
